			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
				(justify left)
			)
		)
		(property "Note" "CAN common-mode choke, 51 uH, AEC-Q200 +125C, body 4.95 x 3.18 x 3.0 mm. WIRING RULE - both bus lines must enter at the SAME PHYSICAL END of the part. Pins 1 and 4 are the two pads 1.73 mm apart at one end; 2 and 3 are the pair at the other end. The windings run the length, so the 3.68 mm pairs (1-2 and 4-3) ARE the windings and the 1.73 mm pairs are same-end neighbours. Transceiver on 1 and 4, connector on 2 and 3. Same statement as Coilcraft's polarity dots at 1 and 4, but it survives a reader who does not recall the dot convention. Get it wrong - transceiver on 1 and 3 - and the two signals run through the core in OPPOSITE directions: the part becomes a differential-mode choke, common-mode noise passes unfiltered, and 51 uH lands in series with the CAN signal. ERC, DRC and the netlist all look correct; it surfaces only as 'CAN misbehaves' at bring-up. SYMBOL - use the part-specific symbol, NOT KiCad's Device:Filter_EMI_CommonMode. The generic places pins 1 and 3 on one side and treats the windings as 1-2/3-4, so wiring it as drawn produces exactly that error. FOOTPRINT - use stock Inductor_SMD:L_CommonModeChoke_Coilcraft_1812CAN (pad centres 1.73 x 3.68 mm). An UltraLibrarian footprint was tried 2026-08-23 and is WRONG: 3.68 mm across the width where the body is only 3.18 mm, so pads span 4.83 mm and terminals would overlap by ~0.17 mm. VALUE - 513 = 51 x 10^3 nH = 51 uH (EIA three-digit in NANOHENRIES, not uH). Do not 'correct' it. NRC - NR = series/termination, C = 7in reel."
			(at 0 0 0)
			(hide yes)
			(show_name no)
			(do_not_autoplace no)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
				(justify left)
			)
		)
